											INSIGHTS
As the size of the property increases, the price also increases. This shows a strong positive connection between size and price.
Properties in urban areas are the most expensive on average, whereas properties in villages and rural areas are the cheapest.
Homes with more bedrooms usually cost more. This shows that people want bigger houses.
Most properties fall under the Mid-Range category, indicating a balanced market between affordability and luxury.
High-end properties are fewer in number, but they add a lot to the total value of the property market
Newer properties tend to have higher prices compared to older ones, showing a preference for modern housing.
Urban areas lead the real estate market. They have both the highest prices and the largest number of properties for sale.
The size of the property and the neighborhood are the two biggest factors that affect property prices.
The real estate market is clearly divided into three categories: Budget, Mid-Range, and High-End.